[{:db/id 17592186364277 :question/problem "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of files reviewed by an antivirus until the first infected file is found\n- The number of infected files found in a computer\n- The number of crayons randomly picked out from a case before a blue crayon is picked\n\nAssume independent trials with a constant success probability strictly between $0$ and $1$ for each experiment. Replace and mix each crayon before the next draw." :question/worked-solution "A geometric distribution models waiting for the first success in independent Bernoulli trials with constant success probability.\n\n- I counts file checks through the first infected file. Under the stated model, $P(T=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p$ for $k=1,2,\\ldots$.\n- II counts the total infected files in a computer; it does not count trials until the first success.\n- III counts nonblue draws before the first blue crayon. Replacement keeps the probability of blue constant. Under the stated model, $P(F=k)=(1-q)^kq$ for $k=0,1,2,\\ldots$.\n\nI and III are geometric, using the trials and failures conventions respectively. The correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\nThe original source omits the independence and constant-probability assumptions and does not specify crayon replacement. Without replacement, III generally is not geometric; the study wording makes the intended model explicit." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
